# INC-004 — Windows Search Service Interruption

**Incident ID:** INC-004  
**Status:** Resolved  
**Priority:** Medium (Simulated)  
**Category:** Windows Services / Service Interruption  
**Affected System:** WIN11-CLIENT01  
**Operating System:** Windows 11 Pro  
**Affected Service:** Windows Search (`WSearch`)  
**Date:** November 2, 2026

## 1. Incident Description

A controlled Windows Search service interruption was simulated to investigate service availability, examine Windows event logs, and practice service recovery procedures.

## 2. Investigation

- Verified that Windows Search was initially running with the startup type set to **Automatic (Delayed Start)**.
- Intentionally stopped the service using the Windows Services console.
- Investigated the System log in Event Viewer using the Service Control Manager event source.
- Identified an earlier **Event ID 7000**, indicating that Windows Search had previously failed to start because it did not respond within the required time.
- Confirmed that the earlier startup timeout was separate from the simulated interruption.

## 3. Resolution

Restarted Windows Search through the Services console.

Verified the service status using PowerShell:

```powershell
Get-Service -Name WSearch
```

**Result:**

```text
Status   Name      DisplayName
------   ----      -----------
Running  WSearch   Windows Search
```

## 4. Root Cause

**Simulated incident:** The service interruption was caused by intentionally stopping Windows Search for troubleshooting practice.

**Historical Event ID 7000:** The underlying cause of the earlier startup timeout was not determined.

